а ядерну зброю заплановане на 12 січня 2027-го.Конфлікти06.10.20266 жовтня 2026 р.РФ поцілила по мосту через Дніпро в ЗапоріжжіРФ поцілила по мосту через Дніпро в ЗапоріжжіВнаслідок російської атаки постраждали п'ятеро людей, серед яких працівники Червоного Хреста.Конфлікти06.10.20266 жовтня 2026 р.У Московській області палає найбільша нафтобазаУ Московській області палає найбільша нафтобазаМоскву та область в ніч на 6 жовтня атакували 650 БпЛА. Вирує пожежа на ЛВДС \"Володарская\" у Костянтиновому.Конфлікти06.10.20266 жовтня 2026 р.Більше публікаційНа головну сторінкуFooterМедіакомпанія DWПро насанглійськоюПресаанглійськоюDW Global Media ForumанглійськоюDW пропонуєLearn GermanDW AkademieанглійськоюСервісПрийом сигналуанглійськоюFAQанглійськоюКонтактианглійськоюУсі темиОстанні публікаціїB2BПродажі та розповсюдженняанглійськоюПодорожіанглійськоюРекламаанглійськоюПідписуйтесь на DW у соцмережахДодайте DW як пріоритетне джерело в Google© 2026 Deutsche WelleЗахист персональних данихВихідні даніІнформація про вебдоступністьНалаштування захисту даних","title":"Олександр Куницький","url":"https://www.dw.com/uk/олександр-куницький/person-52271458","id":305},{"content_hash":"2c97e0283cd93c43216bead5195d4b3e9cd0123ccb5b29e8ef5bb514657ecd73","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:32:57.388150+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/24/ice-officers-electric-shock-gloves","language":"","text":"Alarm over $20m plan to supply ICE officers with electric-shock gloves This article is more than 1 month oldCivil liberties groups decry plan to use gloves linked to death of Kentucky man allegedly shocked 27 times in county jailSara BraunMon 24 Aug 2026 06.00 EDTLast modified on Tue 25 Aug 2026 04.17 EDTSharePrefer the Guardian on GooglePlans to supply Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) officers with special gloves that can deliver electric shocks have alarmed experts and drawn scrutiny amid some high-profile lawsuits over their use in local correctional facilities.In one extreme case, a 43-year-old man died after allegedly being shocked 40 times by officers with a combination of the gloves and a Taser in a county jail in Kentucky, and the man’s widow is suing authorities.Earlier this month, the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), the parent agency of ICE, published plans to spend up to $20m (£15m) to outfit ICE officers by March of next year with the controversial gloves, which are also used in various police departments and some schools.Civil rights advocates protest that the federal agency’s use of force during Donald Trump’s immigration crackdowns already lacks proper oversight.ICE “is putting these [gloves] in the hands of an agency that has dropped its hiring standards, its training, its background requirements in its hiring spree”, Sarah Mehta, deputy director of government affairs in the equality division of the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU), said.“We’re already seeing the consequences of that in lawless and violent enforcement actions in our streets,” she told the Guardian.“What’s really terrifying is use of them on the streets, potentially to terrify people, to create a chilling effect. But what’s also scary is what [their] uses will be where we can’t see them. We don’t know how they’re going to be used in places like Fort Bliss,”she added, referring to the largest migrant detention facility in the US, where allegations of abuse are legion. The government denies claims of any inhumane conditions.Mehta further said of the gloves: “There are no circumstances where we think their use is appropriate.”The device carries the brand name G.L.O.V.E., which stands for Generated Low Output Voltage Emitter, and is manufactured by Compliant Technologies, a company owned by military veterans and based in Lexington, Kentucky. The glove is known as a conductive distraction and de-escalation device and can only be sold to certain verified entities, such as law enforcement, corrections, security, military, and emergency medical services (EMS) personnel, and requires training. It works by delivering an electric shock when the user pushes a button on the glove and touches or grips bare skin.In one demonstration video, a man gives a brief, light touch for approximately one second to the lower back of a prone volunteer, who jerks his body and cries out: “Ow, fuck”.Shock gloves demoCompliant Technologies has posted multiple video demonstrations on its Instagram page. In one video from November 2025, a woman wearing the glove grips firmly on the exposed forearm of a volunteer man for about two seconds. The man buckles to the ground, shouting expletives and says “Let me go!”“I’m going to comply any time,” the man says from the ground in a half-humorous tone to the team running the demonstration. Jeff Niklaus, founder and CEO of Compliant Technologies, can be seen laughing in the video as the man fell to the ground. “Say that louder,” he says.The man, seemingly a prospective customer, says: “Yeah, we need that [product].”Compliant Technologies did not respond to a series of questions and requests for comment from the Guardian.A DHS spokesperson sent this statement in response to questions: “ICE is constantly assessing the needs of our officers in the field to ensure they have the tools and equipment necessary to safely arrest and remove criminal illegal aliens from our country.“Every decision is made with careful consideration and appropriately reviewed to ensure that any technology ICE utilizes is consistent with all applicable law enforcement policies and standards.”The minimum voltage for the CT-G5 model is 324 volts, and the maximum is 380 volts. A Taser, by contrast, can typically fire prongs from a distance to incapacitate someone with 1,200 to 1,500 volts.The glove user manual instructs wearers to avoid using the product on elderly people, small children, pregnant women and disabled people.The gloves have been deployed to a limited extent in law enforcement, healthcare, and even some educational environments across the country. An estimated 50 police departments across 20 states are using the technology, according to an NPR analysis.In May 2025, the Omaha police department purchased 40 pairs of gloves to be used in public schools for just under $66,000, according to city documents. The department confirmed in a statement that 29 of its 34 school resource officers (SROs) – typically police providing security full time in schools – have trained in use of the gloves and work in public middle and high schools.A representative from the Omaha police department shared with the Guardian the report from an incident where the G.L.O.V.E. was used. The report says a female SRO used the shock glove on a male middle school student based on his “highly aggressive and assaultive behavior”. The officer placed the glove on the student’s forearm twice, once for one second, and then again for two to three seconds, which, the report continued, caused the student to drop to the floor, face down.According to the report, the SRO asked if the student needed medical assistance after being shocked, and he said no.Last week, Omaha police acceded to a request from the school superintendent to discontinue the gloves in Omaha, Nebraska’s largest public school district, NBC reported.In a very different example, Joshua Elswick, formerly detained at the Bullitt county jail in Shepherdsville, near Louisville, Kentucky, sued Lt Ryan Derrough alleging excessive force, after Derrough, a guard, used the gloves on Elswick during pre-trial detention.Elswick, who has paranoid schizophrenia and other serious mental illnesses, according to the lawsuit, was arrested and booked into jail in September 2022 after a domestic dispute involving methamphetamine use. Derrough and another deputy attempted to remove Elswick’s handcuffs and leg shackles before formally taking him into custody, but Elswick was paranoid that the officers were going to kill him and he resisted, whereupon Derrough shocked Elswick with the gloves, according to the lawsuit. The incident can be seen in surveillance footage featured in a report on the case from Kentucky TV station WDRB.The footage shows a tense scene, with the guard wearing bright yellow gloves and using them on Elswick, who is struggling against three guards, and yells out.The Kentucky federal court dismissed Elswick’s lawsuit in June 2025, finding that Derrough’s use of force was warranted, a decision upheld by an appeals court in January.A representative for APB Consulting Solutions, a dealer and training partner for Compliant Technologies’ product, told WDRB in 2022 that agencies undergo a lot of training and that used properly the gloves can prevent further injury – while it is recommended not to apply them for more than 15 seconds.Sara Collins, a lawyer representing Elswick, declined to comment on the specific civil case, but told the Guardian: “With proper training and use, effective, less-lethal force options are much-needed resources in law enforcement. Shock gloves, however, are not a new tool.”“I am troubled by the fact that the overwhelming majority of law enforcement agencies across the country do not use them,” she added, noting that relatively few departments out of 18,000 across the US have bought the gloves.“Feasibility, practicality, training, risk of error, and intent are among my many concerns about their implementation by ICE,” Collins said.Meanwhile, Johnathan Mansfield, who was arrested on a public intoxication charge in 2024, suffered a cardiac arrest two weeks after he was allegedly shocked 27 times with the gloves and 13 times by a Taser at Madison county detention center in Richmond, Kentucky, according to the lawsuit filed by Mansfield’s family against the authorities, claiming wrongful death.The complaint alleges: “An internal investigation by the Madison County Detention Center found that ‘ ... Mr Manfield [sic] suffered a cardiac arrest following the series of Taser and G.L.O.V.E. exposures.’”The internal investigation, as cited in the lawsuit, stated that “four distinct use of force incidents were recorded… resulting in a total of 13 taser exposures – comprising both probes and drive-stun applications – lasting between 23 to 28 seconds. Additionally, there were 27 exposures using the G.L.O.V.E.”One of the shock glove exposures lasted for 45 seconds and another for 99 seconds, the document alleges.The suit, filed in a Madison county, Kentucky circuit court, is still pending.Explore more on these topicsICE (US Immigration and Customs Enforcement)newsShareReuse this content","title":"Alarm over $20m plan to supply ICE officers with electric-shock gloves","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/24/ice-officers-electric-shock-gloves","id":306},{"content_hash":"11206386b8a70d5787fe84653c6646d2abb4a80291c0ab74b4b53888a6f159f0","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:32:57.196674+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/african-architects-challenge-venice-exhibition-to-decolonise-and-start-new-conversations-205948","language":"","text":"African architects challenge Venice exhibition to decolonise and start new conversations Published: May 19, 2023 5:03am EDT https://theconversation.com/african-architects-challenge-venice-exhibition-to-decolonise-and-start-new-conversations-205948 https://theconversation.com/african-architects-challenge-venice-exhibition-to-decolonise-and-start-new-conversations-205948 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Presented since 1979, the Venice Architecture Biennale (La Biennale di Venezia) is possibly the most influential architecture exhibition in the world. For the first time, this year’s edition is curated by an African architect, Lesley Lokko. She has ensured that a strong African presence is the central feature of the show. Indeed, the 2023 exhibition is part of an undeniable shift towards a more just representation in global architecture. The biennale, a cultural institution established as early as 1895, is a manifestation of a world order established by European imperialism. It is an international platform for a network of powerful academic and professional groups, material producers, construction firms, developers and public authorities. They come together in Venice to show and discuss their work. Visitors to the central exhibition, Force Majeure. Simone Padovani/Getty Images The biennale relies heavily on private sponsors and numerous countries host their own pavilions in Venice. While an African curator has no influence over these pavilions, she has ample latitude to determine the shape of the main pavilion and its exhibitions, the Force Majeure and the Dangerous Liaisons sections. As a professor of architecture with a scholarly focus on African cities and non-western architectural forms, I have been attending the preview week in Venice. I believe that the African presence at the event brings a much needed – and complicated – new perspective that needs to shape the future of the biennale. Lesley Lokko and Demas Nwoko In the very first room of this year’s show, at the entrance of the Corderie dell’Arsenale – a thin 300 metre long building where the Venetian navy produced its ropes for over seven centuries – a diffused blue light shines. It invites visitors to reflect on the notion of the blue hour, the time after sunset and before night. For Lokko the light marks a new era: “A moment between dream and awakening … a moment of hope.” Read more: Venice architecture biennale: how pioneering Ghanaian architects reckoned with tropical modernism A Ghanaian-Scottish architect, educator and novelist, Lokko is the first woman of colour to curate the show. In her curatorial statement she highlights the “laboratory of the future”. Rather than a place for scientific experiments, the laboratory needs to be thought of more as a workshop. Here different practitioners can collaboratively test new forms of architecture. In the west, says Lokko, one continues to associate architects as the figures who build buildings. But they do much more, they build society, competency, knowledge, in a rapidly hybridising and interwoven world. Demas Nwoko. Titi Ogufere/La Biennale di Venezia Lokko subverts perspectives. She invites visitors to look at Africa not as a place where western models should be transferred to, but rather from which much can be learned. The decision to award the Golden Lion for Lifetime Achievement to Demas Nwoko, a Nigerian architect and artist born in 1935, is significant to Lokko’s perspective. His relatively few buildings are cited as “forerunners of the sustainable, resource-mindful, and culturally authentic forms of expression now sweeping across the African continent – and the globe”. An example of this is the Dominican Institute and Chapel he completed in Ibadan, Nigeria in 1975. The motifs of a Christian building are reinterpreted through an African sense of place and ornamentation. Lokko’s approach represents a radical shift in the way the biennale operates. It is an important contribution to the creation of genuine “contact zones”: places of productive exchange between people offering different views. This replaces the old arbitrary hierarchies with a reciprocal respect for diversity. The tower above the altar of Nwoko’s chapel in Ibadan, Nigeria. Joseph Conteh/La Biennale di Venezia This year’s event sparked a controversy over visas being denied to African architects. A good starting point for reciprocal respect would be to make it really possible for all to participate and attend, by breaking the barriers imposed by systemic inequalities and xenophobic immigration policies. What’s on show Of the 89, mostly young, participants invited to this year’s show, over half of are from Africa or the diaspora. They are carefully orchestrated in the show’s two main venues, the Giardini and the Arsenale, and six sections. In the main pavilion, where the Force Majeure exhibition sets the scene, a towering installation by Nigerian visual artist Olalekan Jeyifous epitomises the imagery of the African future. His images are powerful spatial metaphors of the relationship between architecture, communities and environment. And the need to repair the damage done by former colonial powers. Installation by Nigeria’s Olalekan Jeyifous. Matteo de Mayda/La Biennale di Venezia In another room, the Oral Archive by Nairobi-based collective Cave Bureau celebrates the oral tradition of passing down knowledge across generations as a way to keep humans in community with the earth. On a multimedia screen three channels overlap. They display conversations with cave-dwelling communities, sequences from the Anthropocene museum, and drawings, maps and models done across vast geological sites. The emphasis on two of Lokko’s overarching biennale themes – decolonisation and decarbonisation – can also be found also in the long, and at times uneven, sequence at the Arsenale. Here the Dangerous Liaisons section is interwoven with the curator’s special projects titled Food, Agriculture and Climate Change; Gender and Geography; Mnemonic; and Guests from the Future. Work by Kenya’s Cave Bureau. Matteo de Mayda/La Biennale di Venezia Here the synthetic landscapes of Nigerian-born film producer and director Michael Uwemedimo are presented. A physical ground made of clay and contaminated by global capitalism is transported from Port Harcourt in Nigeria to Venice. It is a departure point to imagine the future, displayed through AI-generated images on the ceiling. Congolese artist and photographer Sammy Baloji expertly deconstructs the official narration of colonial occupation, suggesting a view of architecture and the human body as traces of social history. This is done by displaying an old colonial Belgian documentary poetically interwoven with film footage taken today. What this all means The 2023 Venice Architecture Biennale is an important and complicated edition, with a necessary message. One can only hope that the event will continue the process of decolonisation started by Lokko after years of absence of confrontation, comparison, and exchange between different positions. A radical rethinking of the biennale, and of the (architectural) world in general, is long overdue. We need a different future. Enter the blue hour. The biennale opens to the public on 20 May and is on until 26 November Architecture Nigeria Venice Architecture Biennale Ghana African architecture Women and girls RandD Decolonialism urban diversity Venice Architecture Biennale 2023 Tomà Berlanda, University of Cape Town Author Tomà Berlanda Professor of Architecture, University of Cape Town Disclosure statement Tomà Berlanda does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Cape Town provides funding as a partner of The Conversation AFRICA. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.7fkcvv95k","title":"African architects challenge Venice exhibition to decolonise and start new conversations","url":"https://theconversation.com/african-architects-challenge-venice-exhibition-to-decolonise-and-start-new-conversations-205948","id":307},{"content_hash":"cabe12c5db34bf44b971ceea890d80e8d1ed045da8509a9a28be0379fdfa1f46","domain":"www.dw.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:32:56.311152+00:00","final_url":"https://www.dw.com/uk/сергій-ромашенко/person-36257239","language":"","text":"Сергій Ромашенко Перейти до змістуПерейти до головного менюПерейти до інших проєктів DWОстанні відеоРегіониУкраїнаНімеччинаЄвропаТемиПрава людиниГеополітикаЕкологіяРубрикиПолітика й економікаКультура й стиль життяУ фокусіВійна Росії проти УкраїниІранУкраїнські біженці в НімеччиніОстанні відеоСергій РомашенкоЖурналіст Deutsche We