b54871be4243f4be3bb7b8be2f291","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:54.960453+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/china-in-africa-kenya-railway-study-shows-investment-projects-arent-a-one-way-street-205509","language":"","text":"China in Africa: Kenya railway study shows investment projects aren’t a one‑way street Published: May 29, 2023 8:30am EDT https://theconversation.com/china-in-africa-kenya-railway-study-shows-investment-projects-arent-a-one-way-street-205509 https://theconversation.com/china-in-africa-kenya-railway-study-shows-investment-projects-arent-a-one-way-street-205509 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article China is an important economic player in Africa. In 2021 alone, China accounted for nearly US$5 billion in foreign direct investment in African countries. The rapidly increasing Chinese presence across Africa has become a contentious issue both for Beijing and African governments. In particular, mega projects funded by China have resulted in public controversies about the relationship between external investments and public debt. China is Africa’s biggest bilateral lender. In 2020, it held over US$73 billion of Africa’s public debt and nearly US$9 billion of its private debt. Due to this, US Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen has accused China of leaving countries “trapped in debt”. Kenya has been no exception. China’s involvement in the construction of Kenya’s Standard Gauge Railway is a typical example of controversies brought by China-supported investments. These include issues of increasing socio-economic inequalities between different population groups advanced by large-scale investments, local labour mistreatment by Chinese managers, accusations of neo-colonialism, and the long-term sustainability of loans issued by the Exim Bank of China for projects. In 2022, with a total debt of US$6.83 billion, China was Kenya’s biggest bilateral creditor. Out of this amount, US$5.3 billion was advanced by the Exim Bank of China to finance the Standard Gauge Railway. It is against this background that our study asked if Chinese actors indeed determined how mega-infrastructures are realised in African countries. We examined the specific ways in which Chinese state-owned enterprises are involved in the construction of Kenya’s Standard Gauge Railway. We analysed how infrastructure development was realised on the ground and how Chinese construction companies shaped the process. The study showed that the decisions of Chinese state-owned enterprises in Kenya do not necessarily present a grand Chinese strategy. Instead, they result from changing political and economic circumstances in China, and reflect both state and private Chinese interests. Acknowledging these dynamics is important because it demonstrates how narratives about China’s involvement in mega-infrastructure development might overemphasise the power of the Chinese state. Simultaneously, this highlights that African governments have more power to influence their industrial development and the sustainability of large-scale projects than mainstream narratives acknowledge. Flagship projects Alongside other large projects, such as the Lamu Port-South Sudan-Ethiopia Transport Corridor, the Standard Gauge Railway is central to Kenya’s national development programme Vision 2030. This is supposed to industrialise the country and advance socio-economic development. But the sustainability of the railway project and its contribution to government debt has been widely debated. In 2022, according to the National Treasury, Kenya’s debt stood at KSh9.15 trillion (US$74.1 billion), equivalent to 67% of the country’s GDP. There are also concerns whether Chinese contracts protect national interests. We took a closer look at the project to see if these fears were well founded. Between May 2019 and September 2020, we conducted interviews during multiple visits to Chinese construction camps alongside the railway construction sites. We interviewed managers and employees in construction and operational departments of China Road and Bridge Corporation, the main railway project contractor. We interviewed informants from the public sector in Kenya, including from Kenya Railways Corporation and Kenya Ports Authority. We also spoke to local government workers, private sector representatives, lawyers and scholars. Our research is unique because we directly engaged with the Chinese actors that built Kenya’s new railway. Their perspectives have been lacking in both public and academic debates. This is because public engagement of Chinese contractors is usually strictly guarded due to the state ownership of these enterprises. Our interviews revealed that in Kenya, China Road and Bridge Corporation constantly shifted its strategies. It also adapted to local circumstances in the country and across East Africa, rather than only imposing its strategic priorities. This compromised its own interests of economic productivity and its public image. Our finding runs counter to any grand visions of transformative infrastructure development, the lens through which Kenya’s rail project has been interpreted. The trade-offs We found that the Chinese entity had adopted a method called the “Early Entry Scheme” to resolve issues of delayed land compensation. This involved direct, case-by-case negotiated payments to landowners. As a result, owners vacated land for project construction before the land settlement was officially approved by the National Land Commission of Kenya. This is uncommon among international contractors. Land compensation for a national infrastructure project is usually a responsibility of national governments. But with the delayed national compensation process, the China Road and Bridge Corporation resorted to the Early Entry Scheme. In Kenya, this scheme was driven by various concerns. Cost-saving was one. The Chinese company had learnt from the first phase of the project that the late delivery of even a small parcel of land could raise the cost of the project if labour and equipment were idle. Another concern was political. For a flagship project funded by the Chinese government, on-time delivery was crucial to promote China’s image as an efficient development partner. Another interesting aspect of the project was how the Chinese company became the main operator of the Standard Gauge Railway – not just the construction contractor. According to our interviews, operating the railway would not benefit the company financially. But the stakes were too high to leave it to chance. Operational challenges that a new company could experience might have affected the public image of the project, as well as the corporation itself. Therefore, the company had to balance its short-term financial interests with long-term reputational concerns. So far, there hasn’t been clear evidence of the Standard Gauge Railway contributing to Kenya’s national economic development. The current investment in the railway between Mombasa and Naivasha (120km away from Nairobi) is not enough to boost the economy. This could only be realised if the railway connected global maritime trade to the hinterland of East Africa, to accelerate transport efficiency at a regional scale. But the Kenyan and Ugandan governments did not manage to agree on financing terms to extend the project. For this reason, in 2018, the Exim Bank discontinued funding for extending Kenya’s railway line to Uganda. This shows that Beijing’s strategies of infrastructure development are not set in stone but change, and can even be reversed, due to shifting circumstances in overseas regions. Still, there are clear winners. Though the long-term profitability of Kenya’s Standard Gauge Railway remains in question, China Road and Bridge Corporation managed to enhance its global market position. In Kenya alone, despite the controversies that surround the new railway, the corporation was given new tenders to complete other key national projects, such as the Nairobi Expressway. As we show in our study, this is not necessarily an outcome of a grand strategy in Beijing. Instead, this is a result of dynamic and ever-changing efforts of Chinese companies that try to align multiple demands between their own economic interests and various political priorities in China and across Africa. This highlights that African countries are not passive recipients of Chinese-funded projects. They have an important role to play in counterbalancing Chinese actors to shape how these projects are realised on the ground. China Debt Africa Chinese investment Debt ceiling Kenya Peacebuilding debt trap BRI Standard Gauge Rail China Road and Bridge Corporation Gediminas Lesutis, University of Amsterdam, Zhengli Huang, Tongji University Authors Gediminas Lesutis Marie Curie Fellow, University of Amsterdam Zhengli Huang Post-doctoral Researcher, Tongji University Disclosure statement Gediminas Lesutis receives funding from the Horizon 2020, Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Individual Fellowship (Project ID: 101023118). Zhengli Huang does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.w9exvrv34","title":"China in Africa: Kenya railway study shows investment projects aren’t a one‑way street","url":"https://theconversation.com/china-in-africa-kenya-railway-study-shows-investment-projects-arent-a-one-way-street-205509","id":410},{"content_hash":"002c8529988b4401f8843e43eadd10d74b86fb043dc847f107ac812dd4b89b2c","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:53.699127+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/african-migration-to-europe-forced-returns-run-into-resistance-206131","language":"","text":"African migration to Europe: forced returns run into resistance Published: May 29, 2023 9:09am EDT Forced return of irregular African migrants from Europe is affecting political relationships in the two regions. GettyImages https://theconversation.com/african-migration-to-europe-forced-returns-run-into-resistance-206131 https://theconversation.com/african-migration-to-europe-forced-returns-run-into-resistance-206131 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The forced return of migrants from the European Union (EU) to their countries of origin continues to be a source of major tension in African countries as well as EU member states. The result has been that the EU – and individual member states – have been scrambling to come up with ways to improve cooperation on migration. What EU countries want is for countries of origin to take back migrants that are not able to live legally in Europe. But return rates from the EU to Africa are the lowest compared to other world regions and have even decreased in the last decade. For example, only 9% of Senegalese with an order to return from the EU did so between 2015 and 2019. In other words, of the 30,650 Senegalese migrants who received an order to leave from a European country between 2015 and 2019, only 2,805 did. Forced return necessitates the cooperation of countries of origin through for example issuing travel documents or allowing flights to land. Based on my recent work and broader research project, I argue that returns are so low in part because European policy makers ignore the competing issues and interests African stakeholders face. I also show how West African states resist cooperating on returns. Their strategies range from reluctant compliance with deportations of their citizens to proactive noncompliance. My findings help explain why current migration partnerships continue to have little effect on returns. Migration partnerships coming up empty Apart from Rwanda, which has signed a deal with the UK to take in asylum seekers, not yet implemented, returns from European countries are hugely unpopular in most African countries. The EU’s Partnership Framework was established in June 2016. It sought to mobilise the instruments, resources and influence of both the EU and member states to establish cooperation with partner countries in order to “manage” migration flows. It stated that the priority was to achieve fast returns. It named five priority countries for this approach: Ethiopia, Mali, Niger, Nigeria, and Senegal, seeking to establish tailored migration compacts with them. This framework has so far largely failed to achieve better migration cooperation. The same applies to the New Pact on Migration and Asylum from 2020, and a revised visa code which allows visa restrictions for countries not adequately cooperating on returns. Forced returns, especially back to west Africa, remain low as noted above. There are a number of reasons for this. Firstly, deportations are often violent and traumatic events for those being returned and involve serious human rights violations. For example, a Senegalese migrant rights group recently documented a case of a person who was deported from a closed psychiatric institution in Germany without medication, medical records or telephone and other personal belongings and was left to fend for himself on arrival. Governments wanting to take care of their citizens will want to avoid this. Secondly, returns can come at the cost of important remittances (often higher than development aid). For example, Nigeria is the largest net remittance-recipient country in sub-Saharan Africa. In 2017, the country received US$22 billion in official remittances, representing 5.9% of Nigeria’s GDP. In comparison, official development assistance came to US$3.36 billion, only 0.89% of GDP. Thus, countries calculate that returns could reduce remittances. And reintegrating deportees comes with social and economic costs. Thirdly, countries feel that they are badly treated by their European counterparts. Legal migration pathways are limited and the EU approach has become increasingly punitive. There are stricter visa conditions for countries not seen to be adequately cooperating on returns, like The Gambia or Senegal. Governments respond in various ways to return pressures from the EU. A range of responses The research considered the interests of governments in Nigeria, Senegal and The Gambia. It built on 129 interviews with policymakers, politicians, civil society activists and academic experts in these countries and in the EU. Countries’ responses range from reluctant compliance to reactive and proactive noncompliance. They are likely to use all these strategies at different times, sometimes simultaneously. Responses are influenced by the often conflicting pressures governments face domestically and externally. Reluctant compliance is when countries comply with deportations, but only reluctantly. For example informal return agreements signal compliance to international partners but attract less scrutiny from parliamentary oversight bodies or the general public. This strategy can, however, backfire and cause distrust and outrage from citizens. Reactive noncompliance includes calling out technical issues and causing delays in return operations. States may fail to identify whether migrants are their citizens, or fail to issue travel documents of migrants awaiting deportation. This can be a less costly strategy than outright refusing deportations, while increasing domestic approval. Proactive noncompliance is the most extreme response. This is when states are more direct in their refusal to cooperate with returns. It includes, for example, stalling on negotiations for a formal return agreement. Senegal and Nigeria have taken this route. The Gambia even implemented a moratorium on all (chartered) deportation flights for a few months. Proactive noncompliance holds the most potential for governments to improve their domestic legitimacy – especially during elections. But it can come at the cost of international support. In the case of The Gambia, for example, the EU has imposed visa sanctions. Way forward EU stakeholders could do more to consider the interests that African countries have when it comes to accepting returnees. Ratcheting up the pressure will produce more resistance. Rather, the EU should focus on repairing relationships. One way would be to offer migration pathways that are accessible, visible and credible. Tying this to return pressures is not likely to work, and will not improve relationships in the long run. Migration European Union (EU) Nigeria Ethiopia Rwanda Deportation West Africa Resettlement Senegal Peacebuilding Niger The Gambia Irregular migration United Kingdom (UK) Franzisca Zanker, Arnold Bergstraesser Institute Author Franzisca Zanker Senior research fellow, Arnold Bergstraesser Institute Disclosure statement This article was written based on a research project in the Mercator Dialogue on Asylum and Migration, funded by the Mercator Stiftung. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.9x945hk33","title":"African migration to Europe: forced returns run into resistance","url":"https://theconversation.com/african-migration-to-europe-forced-returns-run-into-resistance-206131","id":411},{"content_hash":"e490fb21c1ede3b956083646456e7f8b1d11c0d2667ec8e5790b42bc2aa34b08","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:53.653709+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/01/el-nino-orangutans-indonesia-wildlife-destruction-aoe","language":"","text":"‘The toll is huge’: El Niño-driven wildfires overwhelm Indonesia’s orangutan populationThe global climate phenomenon has triggered devastating infernos in the ape’s Indonesian heartlands, with long-term effects on the animals still unknownSupported byAbout this contentRebecca RatcliffeWed 30 Sep 2026 23.00 EDTLast modified on Wed 30 Sep 2026 23.01 EDTSharePrefer the Guardian on GoogleThe mother’s hands were peeling from walking on the torched ground. Her eight-month-old baby, which clung to her side, was dehydrated and thin. The pair, named Mama Abu and Abu (“ash” in Indonesian), were found in an oil palm subsidiary estate, rescuers say.The orangutan pair were saved last month from wildfires that are devouring Indonesia’s forests and peatland, unleashing thick smog, and destroying the habitat of some of the planet’s most vulnerable species.Primate specialists from the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) have called for urgent international assistance to help control the fires, warning this week that thousands of orangutans could be killed, injured or displaced.Wildfires happen every year in Indonesia, where fire is often used to clear land to make way for palm or pulp plantations. But the arrival of a supercharged El Niño has brought especially dry conditions, causing blazes to spread rapidly across parts of Sumatra, Kalimantan (the Indonesian part of Borneo) and Papua.The fires have also taken hold across peatland, which is extremely hard to extinguish as it smoulders underground. In its natural state, it should be waterlogged, but over recent decades, vast areas have been drained by industrial agriculture, leaving it highly flammable.Habitat containing 52% of the total Bornean orangutan population is now affected by the fires, according to a statement from the IUCN’s primate specialists.They are not the only animals affected, however. Blazes have also spread into the habitats of five gibbon species, all of which are listed as endangered on the IUCN Red List. Geopix, an environmental NGO, warned earlier this month that 4,500 fires had been detected across 21 Sumatran elephant habitats over recent months.View image in fullscreenA fire burning on peatland and forest in Sungai Putri village, West Kalimantan, Indonesia, in August. Photograph: Garry Lotulung/The Guardian“I believe that the toll on wild animals being burned, dead, because of the fires is huge,” says Dr Karmele Llano Sanchez, a veterinarian and co-founder and CEO of Yiari, a non-profit focused on primate and habitat conservation, which rescues orangutans and other species from torched forests.View image in fullscreenDr Karmele Llano Sanchez in a rescue operation for orangutans caught up in wildfires in Pemangkat village in West Kalimantan province. Photograph: Muffidz Ma’Sum/Yiari/AFP/Getty ImagesEven animals that survive the catastrophe can face long-term consequences. Food availability falls in the aftermath of fires as forests struggle to recover, and the risk of conflict between animals can rise if they are displaced and forced to live closer to each other. Experts believe that, as with humans, there will also be health impacts for animals that have lived with months of dense smog.So far, more than 20 orangutans, as well as other species such as pangolin, have been evacuated from the fires by Yiari, which is working alongside West Kalimantan’s Natural Resources Conservation Agency, searching the forests for animals in need and responding to reports from the public.One of the rescued orangutans died, probably due to smoke inhalation.Others have been found with burns on their hands after touching the scorching ground, or are hungry and dehydrated.“It hasn’t rained in 80 days here, and everything is dried and burned,” says Sanchez. “[There are] no leaves and no fruits – nothing they can eat.”Animals that are evacuated are given sedatives through a dart, which can be fired as high as 30 metres into the trees. A team of about 10 people will hold a net to catch the animal as it tumbles from the branches. An adult male orangutan can weigh about 70kg.View image in fullscreenRescuers hold a net to catch a sedated orangutan as it comes down from the trees. Photograph: Muffidz Ma’Sum/Yiari/AFP/Getty ImagesIf a mother is carrying very young offspring, they usually fall together, with the baby clinging to its mother.Such evacuations are a last resort, says Sanchez, undertaken when the area of forest is destroyed: “Otherwise they will for sure die.”Indonesian wildfires threaten leading orangutan research site in BorneoRead moreThere are many animals in peril that simply can’t be reached: proboscis monkeys, which are endangered, Sunda pangolin and snakes – including a python and king cobra – have all been found dead, according to local media reports.Forestry ​minister Raja Juli Antoni told media the wildfires could continue until early November, with the rainy season expected to arrive late. An estimated 600,000 hectares (1.4 million acres) were damaged by fire in August, according to the environmental group YKAN.View image in fullscreenA Nasa satellite of Borneo with red dots indicating where fires have been detected. Peat fires are a recurring challenge in Indonesia. Photograph: Modis/NasaResearchers have observed that large fire events have been followed by unusually high litterfall, where leaves and small branches drop from trees. This is probably because smog has blocked sunlight, preventing photosynthesis, says Dr Susan Cheyne, co-director of Borneo Nature Foundation International, which has conducted studies on the trend.“Even though the fires are out at this point there is still a knock-on effect on food,” says Cheyne, who is also vice-chair for the International Union for Conservation of Nature primate specialist group section on small apes.Such die-offs of leaves occur just as dry season returns, just when species such as orangutan need to eat more, as they rely on flowers and leaves rather than the sugary fruits and pulp that are abundant during the rainy season.Erin Vogel, professor at Rutgers University in New Jersey, says the forest can take years to recover, with trees producing less fruit afterwards, possibly due to a decline in pollinators.In the longer term, tree diversity across burned peat forests can remain suppressed even 12 years on, research has shown. If fires recur, this risks trapping forests in a permanently degraded state.View image in fullscreenMama Abu’s scorched hands after her rescue from forest fires in West Kalimantan. Photograph: Muffidz Ma’sum/Yiari-Ministry of ForestryThe full extent of the long-term impact of the fires on surviving orangutans’ health is unknown. During smoky periods orangutans show ketosis, a sign of increased fat breakdown. It’s believed this is because the smoke raises energy demands, probably by forcing the immune system to work harder.Research published after the 2015 fires found orangutans called less in heavy smoke, and that they had reduced vocal quality. Most vocal changes persisted for two months after the smoke had cleared and probably signalled changes in health, researchers concluded.Gibbons, which rely on song to attract mates, cement bonds and defend territory, have also been found to sing less when exposed to heavy smoke.“We know how [smog] impacts humans in the long term – it can lead to medical problems, breathing problems, asthma, cancer, infections, future lung capacity issues,” says Vogel. “So, I think, there’s no reason to predict that it wouldn’t affect them the same way.”Further studies are needed, but for now, research sites themselves are threatened by the flames. Both the Tuanan orangutan research station in Central Kalimantan, and Rangkong research station in West Kalimantan, have been threatened by fires in recent weeks. Staff and local people have worked to halt the spread, setting up improvised sprinkler systems and clearing the ground cover. Localised rains have brought some relief at Rangkong, though staff fear this may only be temporary. At Tuanan, where there is still no rain, the fire fighting continues day and night.View image in fullscreenConservation agency and Yiari staff evacuating a mother orangutan and her baby trapped by smoke in a palm oil plantation in West Kalimantan. Photograph: Yiari/AFP/Getty ImagesWhat is killing Sumatra’s elephants? The battle to save one of our rarest animals Read moreDecades of research are at risk, says Vogel, who is co-director of the Tuanan orangutan research programme. Academics there have studied the same trees, 2,500 in total, and the same individual orangutans for 23 years, accumulating a crucial body of long-term data.The IUCN says “urgent technical and financial support” are needed, but that the emergency response must be followed by greater investment in re-wetting peatland, addressing deforestation, and ensuring burned forests are restored, not converted into plantations.View image in fullscreenMama Abu is treated by Yiari rescuers after she and her baby were trapped by smoke from forest fires. Photograph: Muffidz Ma’sum/Yiari-Ministry of ForestryFor now, rescuers continue to search the forests for wildlife at risk. The mother and baby rescued last week are now being held at a quarantine centre run by Yiari.When it is safe, they will be released in a new location – one with enough food and no other orangutan populations that might compete with them for territory. “We need to look for a location that is safe, that is a good forest,” says Sanchez. “It is not easy. But we are trying to do our best to find a place that is suitable for them.”Find more age of extinction coverage here, and follow biodiversity reporters Rebecca Ratcliffe, Phoebe Weston and Patrick Greenfield on the app for all the latest news and featuresExplore more on these topicsIndonesiaThe age of extinctionWildfiresEl Niño southern oscillationClimate crisisPrimatologyWildlifeAsia PacificfeaturesShareReuse this content","title":"‘The toll is huge’: El Niño-driven wildfires overwhelm Indonesia’s orangutan population","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/01/el-nino-orangutans-indonesia-wildlife-destruction-aoe","id":412},{"content_hash":"d0f800a36f7ede2b878323b7a9c8c90388b07173cff02400e93425799aae328a","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:52.459554+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/21/indonesia-borneo-sumatra-fires-smoke-toxic-air","language":"","text":"An aerial view of wildfires burning in Sungai Putri, Ketapang regency, West Kalimantan province in Indonesia. Photograph: Willy Kurniawan/ReutersIntensified by this year’s super El Niño, fires in Borneo and Sumatra are emitting toxic air for swathes of the population in Indonesia and its neighboursBy Kate LambSun 20 Sep 2026 23.14 EDTLast modified on Mon 21 Sep 2026 22.46 EDTSharePrefer the Guardian on GoogleFor months, parts of Indonesia’s Borneo and Sumatra islands have been ablaze. The fires are sometimes lit illegally as a cheap way to clear land for plantations of oil palm, of which Indonesia is the world’s largest producer.So prevalent is the technique that it even has a special term: slash and burn.Many of the fires begin as small-scale blazes. But in hot, dry conditions, can easily spiral out of control, sending toxic smoke across borders and affecting the air quality of millions in Indonesia and beyond.An aerial view shows a fire burning in Sungai Putri villageThis year a “super” El Niño could inflame the problem.David Gaveau, a landscape ecologist who runs Nusantara Atlas, which tracks fires and deforestation in Asia, says the worst fires occur on drained and dried out peatland.smoke mapThis type of fire is extremely difficult to extinguish, and can burn for weeks underground.“They go underground, they resurface somewhere else. And then the more water you give it, the more smoke there is,” he says, “These fires are really the legacy of 50 years of forest degradation, deforestation, and particularly on peatland.”If there was an “apex predator” among fires, tropical peatland fires would be a top contender, says Nasa.Fires occur annually in Indonesia, but data from this year indicates it is already looking like a bad year, with the worst hit areas in Sumatra, Kalimantan, the Indonesian part of Borneo, and Papua.Since January at least 895,657 hectares have been burned Indonesia-wide, 34% worse than 2019, according to data from Nusantara Atlas.Area burned by wildfires“It’s trending far higher than 2019 or even 2023,” says Gaveau, “What that means is that this is a terrible year.”In recent weeks, photos from Kalimantan have shown a thick, sepia-toned haze shrouding streets and obscuring cities, schoolchildren wearing masks at their desks, and even rescuers giving oxygen to smoke-affected orangutans.View image in fullscreenMotorcyclists ride through smoke haze from forest and land fires in Palangkaraya city, Central Kalimantan. Photograph: REINA/AFP/Getty Images“Ever since the haze hit, we haven’t opened our doors or windows in a long time. The wind carries smoke from the fires all the way to our porch. Ash and smoke seep directly into the house, and the conditions worsen at night,” Ely Marlina, a 46-year-old schoolteacher and volunteer firefighter told the Guardian.“While we sleep, the whole family is just breathing in the smoke,” she says. “The physical toll is visceral; eyes are left stinging and weeping daily … It feels like if we keep breathing in this smog, the dizziness will take over and our bodies will just give out.”As the fires rage, schools have closed, and emergency rooms have been inundated with patients.View image in fullscreenThis aerial photo shows smoke from wildfires smothering Lanjak village in Kapuas Hulu, West Kalimantan. Photograph: Bay Ismoyo/AFP/Getty ImagesThe numbers of people suffering wildfire-related respiratory illnesses in those provinces had more than doubled to 113,336 by 9 September, from 50,891 on 1 September.Fire emissions from Indonesia in the first week of September reached 19.7m metric tons, accounting for more than a third of the global total, according to Fire Emissions Watch, a portal developed by the European Union’s Copernicus climate monitoring service.View image in fullscreenA man carrying a water bucket walks out of the haze as wildfires approach his house in Ogan Ilir, South Sumatra, Indonesia. Photograph: Mast Irham/EPAThe air quality index in Indonesia and its neighbours shows just how much the fires have become a public health issue.According to air quality index ratings, a healthy level of air, signified by green, is ranked between 0 and 50.Drone footage of a fire in West Kalimantan provinceAnything above 300 is considered hazardous.Every day this September, the air quality in Sampit, central Kalimantan, has been classified as hazardous, going beyond the red and into the worst rating of maroon, according to the Swiss air monitor IQAir. On 10 September, the rating hit 1142.In Kuching, the biggest city on Malaysian Borneo, this month, AQI levels have fluctuated between unhealthy and hazardous.One resident in Kalimantan recently told the Guardian that taking a breath feels like “smoking a cigarette”.When the smoke from these fires crosses borders, it’s called “transboundary haze”.This year, the haze has again plagued Indonesia’s neighbours, blanketing parts of Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei, and even the Philippines. oCarried by monsoonal winds the smoke travelled as far as Manila, with residents urged to stay indoors as much as possible, and to wear mask outdoors.View image in fullscreenThese two photos of the Sarawak Legislative Assembly building in Kuching, on the island of Borneo, taken 17 June 2025 (top), and 5 September 2026, show how thick the smoke haze has become. Photograph: Mohd Rasfan/AFP/Getty ImagesAmong the countries hardest-hit, Malaysia has urged the Association of Southeast Asian Nations to review an agreement on transboundary haze from 2003.Indonesia’s environment ministry is investigating 19 companies allegedly linked to the fires, while police have arrested 113 people on suspicion of lighting them.But enforcement has proven a challenge for the country.View image in fullscreenA water-bombing helicopter releases thousands of litres of water to suppress a peatland fire in Ogan Ilir, South Sumatra. Photograph: Bagus Kurniawan/NurPhoto/ShutterstockThe government has vowed to crackdown on bad actors; it has also deployed planes, helicopters, drones and the military to fight the fires; and it has bolstered mitigation efforts.Malaysia has offered three helicopters, â an aircraft and 52 personnel to assist, while Japan has also sent in helicopters and a fire-fighting force.Indonesian air quality Residents are also doing their bit. A team of female firefighters called the Power of Mama conduct daily surveillance activities, fire monitoring and community outreach, including encouraging people not to burn and warning them of the risks.In West Kalimantan, volunteers are going door-to-door to hand out cylinders of oxygen to residents and firefighters ‌struggling to breathe, in a campaign called “Rumah Oksigen”, or Oxygen House.But the next few weeks will be crucial.With this year’s super El Niño, experts say the fires could peak this September, and continue until November, when the rainy season is expected to start, but the phenomenon is not the only factor.“El Niño is an enabler. Of course the more powerful it is, the longer and the more intense the drought, and that’s not good news,” says Gaveau, “But you need a match. And we know what that is; it’s people.”View image in fullscreenFire trucks parked near a wildfire burning through a peatland field in Sampit, Central Kalimantan, Indonesia, in August. Photograph: Andri Rizky Agustian/APIndonesia’s forestry minister Raja Juli Antoni has also warned that the country is entering a critical phase.“We have not yet escaped the threat of El Niño,” he said. “We are not safe from forest and land fires during this El Niño period. September is our month of struggle, our field of struggle.”Additional reporting by Garry LotulungExplore more on these topicsIndonesiaWildfiresPalm oilAsia PacificfeaturesShareReuse this content","title":"‘The physical toll is visceral’: How Indonesia’s fire haze affects millions - visual guide","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/21/indonesia-borneo-sumatra-fires-smoke-toxic-air","id":413},{"content_hash":"7da48cd13cf48cf4feb99f90266bc13e85caab9ab881b9101d6d3d315846d4e1","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:52.349719+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/nigerias-security-situation-has-got-worse-what-tinubus-administration-needs-to-do-about-it-206545","language":"","text":"Nigeria’s security situation has got worse: what Tinubu’s administration needs to do about it Published: June 4, 2023 3:47am EDT https://theconversation.com/nigerias-security-situation-has-got-worse-what-tinubus-administration-needs-to-do-about-it-206545 https://theconversation.com/nigerias-security-situation-has-got-worse-what-tinubus-administration-needs-to-do-about-it-206545 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Nigeria’s new president, Bola Ahmed Tinubu, has promised to make security a top priority of his administration. In his inaugural speech he promised, among other things, to provide security personnel with better training, equipment, pay and firepower. The new president has read the mood in the country well. A recent survey showed that 77% of Nigerians felt unsafe in their country. The security situation in Nigeria has deteriorated over the past eight years. Every region of the country is affected. Data compiled by this author, from the Armed Conflict Location & Event Data Project shows that there were 59,279 fatalities from security related incidents between 29 May 2015 when President Buhari assumed office and 18 May 2023. This is opposed to 34,066 of such fatalities recorded between 29 May 2007 and 28 May 2015. (See table 1) Source: Armed Conflict Location and Event Data (ACLED) Boko Haram, which has existed in various forms since the 1990s, was for many years the country’s biggest security challenge. The insurgency, which started in Borno State, was concentrated in the north-east region. But now, other non-state armed groups are spreading violence all over Nigeria. At least 7,222 Nigerians were killed and 3,823 abducted as a result of 2,840 violent incidents between January and July 2022. Buhari made a number of promises to address Nigeria’s chronic security problems. But he failed to effectively address them. As a sociologist with specialisation in civil-military relations, armed groups, criminology and security studies, peace and conflict management and human rights, I have been researching various aspects of insecurity in Nigeria. Based on this work, I outline urgent steps the Tinubu administration must take to turn the tide of insecurity. It needs to audit the security budget allocation of its predecessor and take a close look at its implementation. This will help identify areas of inefficiency and corruption. There is also a need for enhanced oversight and coordination among security agencies. On top of this, the Tinubu administration needs to address underlying socio-economic factors that contribute to insecurity. Investment in education, healthcare and infrastructure development are crucial. So are job creation, poverty alleviation and inclusive economic growth. Deteriorating situation Buhari’s administration got off to a strong start in 2015 because it was able to secure arms from the US. This made an impact on the capacity of terror and insurgent groups in the north-east. But, over his two terms, things got worse in every part of the country. North-east Nigeria: ISWAP (Islamic State West Africa Province), in particular, remained resilient by continuously altering its operational strategies – using unmanned aerial vehicles, for example. The group has attacked military and civilian targets in north-west Nigeria and remains a threat in the north-east. The middle belt: This comprises states in the north-central and parts of north-west and north-east Nigeria. In Plateau State, Benue State and southern Kaduna, rural communities face attacks from ethnic militias identified by survivors as Fulani. Most of the attacks are executed to dislocate locals. They destroy crops and farmlands, kill people and animals, burn houses, and make survivors afraid to return. In 2018, these attacks claimed six times more civilian fatalities than the terrorist insurgency in the north-east. Over 100 settlements in Plateau State and southern Kaduna have been occupied by Fulani groups and renamed after communities were displaced. The issue has been raised in the National Assembly. And in 2020, the Plateau State government enacted a law against kidnapping, land grabbing, cultism and other violence. But attacks continued in Plateau State communities. North-west region: A new wave of banditry emerged in the region during Buhari’s administration. Initially it appeared to be driven by ethnic feuds between Fulani and Hausa groups. But militia groups who had taken refuge in forests also appeared to be involved. Bandits attacked military locations, kidnapped hundreds of school students and abducted travellers. They even bombed a train and abducted passengers. Numerous bandit groups now exist across the north-west region and Niger State. These groups are economically driven. When they are not kidnapping for ransom, they exploit the lack of state presence to impose taxes and protection levies on rural communities. Kidnapping for ransom is currently a blooming criminal enterprise across urban areas in Nigeria and on major road networks. South-east: Here too the situation deteriorated under Buhari. His lopsided appointments – known as Buhari’s 97% vs 5% – fuelled a violent secessionist threat in the region. The Indigenous People of Biafra (IPOB) championed the cause, and its leader, Nnamdi Kanu, was arrested. But the group has remained resilient and, through threats and violence, it enforces days of sit-at-home in parts of the south-east. Prison breaks: Eleven successful breaks between 2017 and 2022 have enabled 6,355 inmates, including high profile terrorists and bandits, to escape. My findings reveal that only 758 people have been rearrested. Four successful breaks occurred during President Goodluck Jonathan’s regime, with 930 escaped and 274 rearrested or returned. New administration’s to-do list The security budget and its implementation must be audited to identify inefficiencies and corruption. Regular audits, citizen participation and whistle-blower protection would improve transparency and accountablity. Security agencies need better oversight and coordination, along with performance monitoring and accountability. These measures can improve the impact of security budgets. It’s also vital to address the socio-economic factors that contribute to insecurity. The country needs job creation, poverty alleviation and inclusive economic growth, particularly in areas affected by insurgency and insecurity. Investment in education, healthcare and infrastructure will create opportunities that discourage criminal activities. Inclusive governance is essential for peaceful coexistence and a sense of belonging. The government must also address the lingering crisis of displacement and occupation in the middle belt, and the secession agitation in the south-east. Effective communication between communities and security agencies is essential. The security sector must respond faster to early warning signs. Finally, perpetrators of violence and their sponsors must be prosecuted. Security Nigeria Insurgency Boko Haram Peacebuilding President Muhammadu Buhari Islamic State West Africa Province (ISWAP) IPOB Bandits Farmer-herder conflict Kidnappping President Bola Ahmed Tinubu Sallek Yaks Musa, University of Northampton Author Sallek Yaks Musa Lecturer, University of Northampton Disclosure statement Sallek Yaks Musa does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AAJ.k3jfhkx7a","title":"Nigeria’s security situation has got worse: what Tinubu’s administration needs to do about it","url":"https://theconversation.com/nigerias-security-situation-has-got-worse-what-tinubus-administration-needs-to-do-about-it-206545","id":414},{"content_hash":"cc06a2bb1bbc154b60bc70aa9d1c884debfde63372f95b306a8ad066bcade007","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-08T07:31:51.488672+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/news/prabowo-subianto","language":"","text":"Prabowo SubiantoSeptember 2026Indonesia gets its first aircraft carrier amid debate over cost and military ambitionsSep 18 2026 00.04 EDTMore than 800 students and teachers in Indonesia suffer food poisoning after eating government free meals Sep 3 2026 01.54 EDTAugust 2026‘Like smoking a cigarette’: Indonesia chokes on haze as blazes intensify amid super ‘El Niño’In Indonesia’s Kalimantan, fires have ravaged tens of thousands of acres, with suffocating smoke reaching Malaysia and putting Singapore on alertAug 25 2026 00.17 EDTFebruary 2026PM praises ‘very significant’ Indonesia security pact – as it happenedThis blog is now closedFeb 6 2026 02.12 ESTNovember 2025Indifferent, nostalgic or plain pragmatic: Indonesia Gen Zs react to strongman Suharto’s national hero statusNov 16 2025 20.32 ESTFury as Indonesia declares late authoritarian ruler Suharto a national heroNov 10 2025 03.56 ESTMaggots in the meals, glass in the rice: Indonesia’s free school meals tainted by food poisoningNov 9 2025 19.30 ESTBritish woman on Bali death row flies home on humanitarian grounds Nov 6 2025 21.32 ESTOctober 2025Indonesia’s new capital, Nusantara, in danger of becoming a ‘ghost city’Oct 28 2025 20.35 EDTBritish woman on death row in Bali for drug smuggling to be repatriatedOct 21 2025 20.20 EDT‘Hot mic’ hot mess: gaffes made by global